              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 4)`, the function is copying 4 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__tmp` to the location pointed to by `&__ret`. If the size of `__ret` is less than 4 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__ret) >= 4) {\n    memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, the size of `__ret` is checked before calling `memcpy` to ensure that it is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be available at a later date. Always refer to the official OWASP and CWE websites for the most up-to-date information."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4af58ba6-da93-3a4e-b916-5d1b7cf8cd3b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(__expected, &__old, 4)`, the function is copying 4 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__old` to the location pointed to by `__expected`. If the size of `__expected` is less than 4 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__expected) >= 4) {\n    memcpy(__expected, &__old, 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, `memcpy` is only called if `__expected` is large enough to hold the data being copied. If it is not, an error is handled instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e01156c-68ef-35eb-b2a4-c37997d383f5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 4)`, if the size of `__val` is more than 4 bytes, it can lead to buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(__val);\nif (size <= 4) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, __val, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first get the size of `__val` and then check if it is less than or equal to 4 before copying it to `__tmp`. If the size of `__val` is more than 4, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `cstring` header file to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e65bccf-72e8-3d13-b790-d690e6889d0f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__old, __expected, 4)`, if the size of `__expected` is more than 4 bytes, it can lead to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `memcpy` is `strncpy` which includes the size of the destination buffer as a parameter. This ensures that the function does not write past the end of the buffer. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(&__old, __expected, sizeof(__old));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the `cstring` library in C++. Therefore, the following library needs to be included:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "59302219-141a-3ead-9b91-c332b4d0a7a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by using functions that limit the amount of data copied to the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11). Additionally, it is important to validate and sanitize all input data to prevent buffer overflow attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memcpy` function with a safer function like `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy_s(&__tmp, sizeof(__tmp), __desired, 4);\n```\n\nThis will ensure that no more than `sizeof(__tmp)` bytes are copied, preventing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "902be792-ee07-3bcd-addc-2e1295145b7b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 4)`, if the size of `__val` is more than 4 bytes, it can lead to buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(__val);\nif (size <= 4) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, __val, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first get the size of `__val` and then check if it is less than or equal to 4 before copying it to `__tmp`. If the size of `__val` is more than 4, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `cstring` header file to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
