            {
              "id": "5e90281b-dfc4-391f-85d2-c3d55ea15994",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `__val` to the location pointed to by `__tmp`. If the size of `__tmp` is less than 8 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__tmp) >= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `memcpy` is only called if `__tmp` is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1aa43bcc-4e78-3d26-9b8b-d3a34541dda2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(__ret, &__tmp, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__tmp` to the location pointed to by `__ret`. If the size of the buffer at `__ret` is less than 8 bytes, this will result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n// Ensure __ret is at least 8 bytes\nif (sizeof(__ret) >= 8) {\n    memcpy_s(__ret, sizeof(__ret), &__tmp, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
